{
  "image_name": "0ac5d9a743d4b273f432b1c0e9f33485.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how palmitoylation-related enzymes regulate membrane protein trafficking and neural development. In the upper schematic, the fatty acid transporter CD36 is shown moving between intracellular compartments and the plasma membrane. CD36 is palmitoylated by ZDHHC enzymes, especially ZDHHC5 at the cell surface or in endosomes, and depalmitoylated by APT1. This lipid modification appears to control whether CD36 resides in membrane nanodomains, is internalized into endosomes, or is returned to the membrane. The diagram also links insulin receptor signaling to this trafficking cycle, suggesting that external signals can stimulate delivery of CD36 from internal stores, while a constitutive route supplies it under baseline conditions. Earlier steps in the secretory pathway are shown in the endoplasmic reticulum and Golgi, where additional ZDHHC family members modify CD36 before export.\n\nThe lower panel broadens the theme to the nervous system, placing different ZDHHC enzymes in specific developmental or functional processes. These include excitatory and inhibitory synapse formation, synaptic plasticity, dendrite and axon outgrowth, clustering of axon initial segment proteins, oligodendrocyte maturation, myelination, and injury-induced axon degeneration. Overall, the figure presents palmitoylation as a key organizer of membrane trafficking and neural cell specialization.",
